From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 10:44:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pdp11: -mfis, using the FIS instructions for single precision
 arithmetic

FIS is the small floating point option of the PDP-11 line: the KE11-F on
the 11/35 and 11/40, the KEV11 on the 11/03.  Four instructions, single
precision only, and no floating point registers at all -- a general
register points at a two-element stack of values, the right operand at
(R) and the left at (R)+4, the result replaces the left operand, and R is
then incremented by 4 so that it points at the result.

So each of addsf3, subsf3, mulsf3 and divsf3 lays its operands out in a
stack temporary, loads its address, and reads the result back; the
instruction itself is one word.  A temporary rather than real pushes,
because pushing would move the stack pointer in the middle of a function
and floats never live in registers on this machine, so the operands
themselves are very often stack addresses whose displacements would then
all need correcting.  The memory traffic is described loosely on purpose:
the insn reads a BLKmode MEM through the pointer, which keeps the stores
of the operands from looking dead, and clobbers all of memory, which
keeps anything from moving across it.  Naming the two slots exactly would
mean writing down stack addresses that reload is still free to rewrite.

Off by default, and -mno-fis turns it back off, because whether the four
instruction codes do anything is a property of the machine rather than of
the processor: a UKNC, for one, has no FIS hardware but emulates the codes
in a HALT-mode trap handler, while elsewhere nothing handles them and the
program dies.  Mutually exclusive with -mfpu, which is an error rather
than a silent choice of one of them.  __pdp11_fis is predefined when the
option is on, next to the existing __pdp11_fpu.

Measured on a UKNC, where the monitor emulates the instructions, at 4602
CPU ticks per single precision addition against 13038 for the library
call, so 2.8 times faster even with the trap; on real KE11-F hardware the
gap is of course much wider.  Correctness does not depend on this option:
a later patch makes the library right for these formats too.
